      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You raise a very valid point about the tension between procedural fairness and distributive justice. A pure lottery provides equal opportunity but ignores essential survival needs, whereas prioritizing vulnerability ensures those who lack stability get the most direct help. True equity in public policy often requires balancing strict neutrality with the moral imperative to assist the most disadvantaged first. Your proposal should explicitly define how many units get automatic priority based on extreme need versus a lottery for those whose circumstances differ slightly. This distinction isn't necessarily social engineering, but rather a calibrated policy that targets limited resources toward the exits they actually need most urgently.",
